WEBVTT

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.970
A ticket arrives. Jev reads it and routes it.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:20.990
Venn, with no history, is given plain descriptions of the 77 queues and routes the same ticket.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:28.990
Scored on the same 3,080 tickets: Jev 79.7%, Venn without history 69.0%. On day one, use Jev.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:35.600
Neither has seen one of this desk's tickets yet.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.700
Attach the desk's closed tickets. Nothing is trained; the store is pointed at.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:51.220
Every answer now comes with the precedents it used, and how each was resolved.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:01:05.750
The counter moves to 93.6%.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:15.910
Five resolved tickets per queue already clear the decision model's interval on this desk; on CLINC150 it takes fifty.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:28.590
One embedding of the ticket. From it: the queue, the three precedents, and whether it belongs in the queue it landed in.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:39.700
A ticket a legacy rule filed in the wrong queue: the same vector, one more question, and set_membership flags it.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:52.840
The registered question, affected by the FX-fee change or not, at threshold 0.9: 5% precision and 9,505 tickets sent to review.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:02:02.840
Re-tag the whole backlog with the 77 queues and file the two FX queues: 361 tickets, 98% precision, 99% recall, 1.9 seconds in process.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:13.990
A sweep is as good as its question. The queues the desk already has are the better question.

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.990
“I was NOT charged twice. One charge, but my statement shows two lines.”

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:24.990
Venn's precedents say duplicate charge. Jev reads the negation.
